title: Adding one
mode: draw
chapter: Arithmetic
check: behaves
parts: not chip:my_half_adder
needs: my_xor my_half_adder
---- intro
Counting means adding 1, over and over, so circuits do it all the time. Adding 1 to a two-bit number `a1 a0` is simpler than a full adder:

- the right column adds 1 to `a0`: the answer bit is NOT `a0`, and it carries when `a0` was 1, so the carry is just `a0`.
- the left column adds that carry to `a1`: a half adder.

The answer can be 3 + 1 = 4, binary **100**, so it has three lights, `y2 y1 y0`.
---- task
Make `y2 y1 y0` the number `a1 a0` plus one.
---- start
input a1 0 0
input a0 0 100
led y2 400 0
led y1 400 70
led y0 400 150
---- reference
input a1, a0
led y2, y1, y0
y0 = ~a0
y1 = a1 ^ a0
y2 = a1 & a0
---- answer
input a1 0 0
input a0 0 100
chip h1 my_half_adder 200 0
not n1 200 150
led y2 400 0
led y1 400 70
led y0 400 150
a1 -> h1.a
a0 -> h1.b
h1.carry -> y2
h1.sum -> y1
a0 -> n1.in
n1 -> y0
---- hint1
`y0` is NOT `a0`. The carry out of the right column is `a0` itself.
---- hint2
A half adder gets `a1` and `a0`: its `sum` is `y1` and its `carry` is `y2`.
---- solution
`y0` = NOT `a0`. The half adder adds `a0` (the carry) to `a1`: `sum` is `y1`, `carry` is `y2`. Try 1 1: the lights show 1 0 0, which is 4.
---- done
An incrementer is a row of half adders, the carry going in at one end. The counters at the end of this course have one inside them.
